<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="mth124-act-quiz1-review">
<title>Activity: Quiz 1 Review</title>

<page>
<assemblage xml:id="q1rev-review-functions">
<title>Function Basics (LT1)</title>
<p>A relation is a <alert>function</alert> if every input in the domain is mapped to exactly one output.</p>
<ul>
<li><p><em>Tables:</em> No <m>x</m>-value can be paired with more than one <m>y</m>-value.</p></li>
<li><p><em>Graphs:</em> The curve must pass the Vertical Line Test.</p></li>
<li><p><em>Notation:</em> In <m>f(x) = y</m>, <m>x</m> is the input and <m>y</m> is the output.</p></li>
</ul>
</assemblage>

<exercise xml:id="q1rev-evaluate" workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Suppose <m>f(x) = 2x^2 - x - 3</m>. Evaluate <m>f(-2)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>7</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>f(-2) = 2(-2)^2 - (-2) - 3 = 8 + 2 - 3 = 7</m>.</p>
<p>Watch for <m>-2^2</m> entered as <m>-4</m> on a calculator, and for <m>-(-2)</m> written as <m>-2</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1rev-meaning" workspace="1.2in">
<statement>
<p>Let <m>V(t)</m> represent the volume of water, in gallons, remaining in a conical tank <m>t</m> minutes after a drainage valve is opened. Write a complete sentence explaining the practical meaning of <m>V(12) = 450</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Twelve minutes after the valve is opened, <m>450</m> gallons of water remain in the tank.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>Twelve minutes after the drainage valve is opened, <m>450</m> gallons of water remain in the tank.</p>
<p>A good sentence names both quantities with units and says when the time is measured from. Watch for answers that say the tank drains <m>450</m> gallons in <m>12</m> minutes.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1rev-tables" workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Below are two relations represented as tables. One of them is a function, and the other is not. Identify which is which, and briefly explain how you know.</p>
<sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto">
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="minor"><cell colspan="6">Relation A</cell></row>
<row><cell right="major"><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell></row>
<row><cell right="major"><m>y</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="minor"><cell colspan="6">Relation B</cell></row>
<row><cell right="major"><m>x</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>4</m></cell></row>
<row><cell right="major"><m>y</m></cell><cell><m>-2</m></cell><cell><m>-1</m></cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>1</m></cell><cell><m>2</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Relation A is a function. Relation B is not.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>In Relation A, every <m>x</m>-value appears once, so each input has exactly one output. Repeated <m>y</m>-values are allowed. In Relation B, the input <m>x = 4</m> is paired with both <m>-2</m> and <m>2</m> (and <m>x = 1</m> with both <m>-1</m> and <m>1</m>), so it is not a function.</p>
<p>Students who check the wrong row will reverse the answer, since the two tables are the same pairs with <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> swapped.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="q1rev-graph">
<introduction>
<p>Use the graph of <m>y = g(x)</m> below to answer each part.</p>
<image width="50%">
<shortdescription>The graph of g, made of three connected line segments.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>The graph of g consists of three connected line segments. The first rises from the point negative four, negative three to the point negative two, five. The second falls from negative two, five to the point two, negative three. The third rises from two, negative three to the point four, one.</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="q1rev-graph-g">
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-5,-4,5,6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<line endpoints="((-4,-3),(-2,5))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="3"/>
<line endpoints="((-2,5),(2,-3))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="3"/>
<line endpoints="((2,-3),(4,1))" stroke="#0072CE" thickness="3"/>
<point p="(-4,-3)" fill="#0072CE" stroke="#0072CE"/>
<point p="(-2,5)" fill="#0072CE" stroke="#0072CE"/>
<point p="(2,-3)" fill="#0072CE" stroke="#0072CE"/>
<point p="(4,1)" fill="#0072CE" stroke="#0072CE"/>
<label anchor="(2.3,4.3)" alignment="east"><m>y = g(x)</m></label>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>

<task workspace="0.8in">
<statement>
<p>Find <m>g(1)</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-1</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>The point on the graph above <m>x = 1</m> is <m>(1, -1)</m>, so <m>g(1) = -1</m>. This value sits in the middle of a segment, so students need to read from the grid rather than a marked point.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="0.8in">
<statement>
<p>Find all values of <m>x</m> for which <m>g(x) = 1</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>x = -3</m>, <m>x = 0</m>, <m>x = 4</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>Draw the horizontal line <m>y = 1</m>. It crosses the graph at <m>(-3, 1)</m>, <m>(0, 1)</m>, and <m>(4, 1)</m>.</p>
<p>A common error is giving the <m>y</m>-values or stopping after the first crossing.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="0.8in">
<statement>
<p>Is <m>g</m> a function? Explain why or why not.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>Yes.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>Yes. Every vertical line crosses the graph at most once, so each input has exactly one output. The fact that a horizontal line crosses three times does not matter here; that is a question about inverses.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<assemblage xml:id="q1rev-review-aroc">
<title>Average Rate of Change (LT2)</title>
<p>The average rate of change measures how much the output changes, on average, for each one-unit increase in the input over an interval <m>[a, b]</m>.
<md><mrow>\text{average rate of change} = \frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x} = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}</mrow></md>
The units are always units of output per unit of input.</p>
</assemblage>

<exercise xml:id="q1rev-aroc-formula" workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of <m>\displaystyle h(t) = \frac{120}{t + 2}</m> over the interval <m>[2, 8]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-3</m></p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>h(2) = \frac{120}{4} = 30</m> and <m>h(8) = \frac{120}{10} = 12</m>, so
<md><mrow>\frac{h(8) - h(2)}{8 - 2} = \frac{12 - 30}{6} = -3.</mrow></md></p>
<p>Students who divide by <m>10</m> (the value of <m>t + 2</m>) instead of <m>8 - 2</m> get <m>-1.8</m>.</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q1rev-coffee">
<introduction>
<p>The temperature <m>T</m>, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh cup of coffee <m>m</m> minutes after it is poured is given in the table below.</p>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
<row><cell right="major"><m>m</m> (minutes)</cell><cell><m>0</m></cell><cell><m>5</m></cell><cell><m>10</m></cell><cell><m>20</m></cell></row>
<row><cell right="major"><m>T(m)</m> (<m>^\circ</m>F)</cell><cell><m>190</m></cell><cell><m>160</m></cell><cell><m>138</m></cell><cell><m>110</m></cell></row>
</tabular>
</introduction>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change of the coffee's temperature on the interval <m>[0, 5]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-6</m> degrees per minute</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>\dfrac{160 - 190}{5 - 0} = \dfrac{-30}{5} = -6</m> degrees Fahrenheit per minute.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Find the average rate of change on the interval <m>[10, 20]</m>.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p><m>-2.8</m> degrees per minute</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p><m>\dfrac{110 - 138}{20 - 10} = \dfrac{-28}{10} = -2.8</m> degrees Fahrenheit per minute.</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>Describe in words the practical meaning of your answer to the previous part.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>From <m>10</m> to <m>20</m> minutes after it is poured, the coffee cools by about <m>2.8</m> degrees per minute on average.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>Between <m>10</m> and <m>20</m> minutes after it is poured, the coffee's temperature drops by an average of <m>2.8^\circ</m>F per minute. Comparing with the first part, the coffee cools more slowly later on.</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>
